# port-patches.sh: glm/parity-r6 @ 1571e7dfdd29 over glm/zig-engine-fixes-on-186 @ e027c49cd06d (commits 7ffb37f42b63..4bab856b97f4); regenerate with scripts/port-patches.sh, do not edit
glm_cuda: the vision tower's workspace for its largest pass is taken at start, before the staging and the pool are sized

The tower took its buffers lazily at the first picture (~0.4 GiB for a pass
of 8,192 patches), outside the startup sizing: the pool and the kept-state
budget were sized from free memory without it, so a first picture could
take room they were promised. Now rank 0 ensures the workspace of its
largest pass right after loading the tower: towerRows, the larger of the
tower's pass_patches and one picture of the most tokens a picture may have
(TENSORFOLD_GLM_IMAGE_TOKENS, 2048 by default, four patches a token; the
frontend's bound is 8000), with the rope tables and the frame upload of
that size. With the defaults that is the lazy allocation the first
8,192-patch picture made before, so a pass never grows it; the startup log
names it. Vision off, or rank 1: unchanged.

Switches: none new. Tests (host): towerRows.

Measured on the pair: not run yet (the startup line, and the pool and
kept-state budget with vision on against off).

Credits: Robert Wojciechowski (GitHub @wojo, X @rwojo).
Apply from the checkout root with: git apply 0029-zig-glm-tower-workspace.patch

